# Vendored llama.cpp + ggml PUBLIC headers — HEADERS ONLY
# Upstream: https://github.com/ggml-org/llama.cpp
# Pinned commit: 687e7789271ec1276e3470f158428e11a4f80b6f
# Released upstream as llama.cpp b10330
#
# WHY THESE ARE VENDORED RATHER THAN DOWNLOADED
#
# scripts/provision-llama.sh fetches the prebuilt dylibs from the upstream
# release asset `llama-b10330-bin-macos-arm64.tar.gz`. That asset ships
# dylibs, CLI executables and LICENSE only — it contains ZERO headers.
#
# The headers exist only in the source tree, and the only downloadable form of
# that is GitHub's auto-generated source archive, which is (a) 36 MB against
# the binary asset's 11 MB and (b) NOT guaranteed byte-stable by GitHub, so a
# committed sha256 for it can break with no upstream release having happened.
#
# Vendoring the 24 headers (~336 KB) keeps provisioning down to one immutable,
# checksummed download, lets a fresh clone build offline, and turns the
# "headers and dylibs must come from the same commit" rule into an invariant
# that is checkable in-tree instead of a download-time coincidence.
#
# HOW THEY REACH THE COMPILER
#
# provision-llama.sh copies this directory into `.llama/b<release>/include/`,
# alongside the `lib/` staged from the release tarball. local-llm/Package.swift
# then passes `-I<prefix>/include`. Keeping the compile-time copy inside the
# prefix means exactly one set of these headers is ever on the include path,
# and it means an explicit `LOCAL_LLAMA_PREFIX` (release packaging, or a
# hand-built llama.cpp) supplies its own matching headers rather than silently
# combining someone else's dylibs with these.
#
# Distinct from Sources/CLlamaBridge/third_party/llama_cpp_common/, which
# vendors the llama.cpp `common/` headers. Those are on the compiler's header
# search path directly because CLlamaBridge.cpp includes them; these are the
# public API headers that `common/` itself includes. BOTH must come from the
# commit recorded above, and both must match the dylibs — the mangled C++
# symbol names do not change across these bumps, so a mismatch fails as silent
# runtime corruption rather than as a link error.
#
# Re-vendor procedure (when bumping the pin, e.g. to b10331):
#
#   # 1. Resolve the commit for the new release tag:
#   gh api repos/ggml-org/llama.cpp/git/refs/tags/b10331 --jq '.object.sha'
#
#   # 2. Fetch and unpack the source archive for that tag:
#   curl -fsSL -o /tmp/llama-src.tar.gz \
#     https://github.com/ggml-org/llama.cpp/archive/refs/tags/b10331.tar.gz
#   rm -rf /tmp/llama-src && mkdir /tmp/llama-src
#   tar -xzf /tmp/llama-src.tar.gz -C /tmp/llama-src
#   SRC=/tmp/llama-src/llama.cpp-b10331
#
#   # 3. Refresh these headers (DEST is the directory containing this file):
#   DEST=local-llm/third_party/llama_cpp_api
#   cp "$SRC"/include/*.h "$DEST/"
#   cp "$SRC"/ggml/include/*.h "$DEST/"
#
#   # 4. Re-vendor common/ too, from the SAME tree — see
#   #    Sources/CLlamaBridge/third_party/llama_cpp_common/COMMIT
#
#   # 5. Update the commit SHA above, in that COMMIT file, and in
#   #    local-llm/LLAMA_CPP_PIN (LLAMA_CPP_COMMIT). provision-llama.sh
#   #    fails closed if the pin and this file disagree.
#
#   # 6. rm -rf .llama && ./scripts/provision-llama.sh
#   #    cd local-llm && swift test   — 21 tests, real inference; catches drift.
